<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\StyloMail.Core\StyloMail.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The protocol line readers, the backend authentication exchange and the credential providers
      are internals: they are how the proxy does its work, not what it offers.

      Their bounds and their failure modes are correctness properties though, and a bound that is
      only exercised through a whole successful session is a bound nobody has ever seen fire. So is
      "the credential never reaches a log" - that can only be tested by driving the code that would
      leak it. This grants the test assembly access so each limit and each failure path can be
      driven directly, rather than widening the public surface of a library whose API should be
      "accept this connection" and "hand back bytes".
    -->
    <AssemblyAttribute Include="System.Runtime.CompilerServices.InternalsVisibleToAttribute">
      <_Parameter1>StyloMail.AccessProxy.Tests</_Parameter1>
    </AssemblyAttribute>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Deliberately no third-party package references.

    Session proxying is the one place in StyloMail where a library's convenience is actively
    dangerous: an IMAP client library that models messages as objects would re-serialise them on
    the way past, re-folding headers and re-encoding bodies, which invalidates DKIM at the
    receiving end and breaks the one promise a mail proxy must keep. "We did not rewrite your
    mail" is a claim we can only make about a byte pump we control. The same reasoning as
    StyloMail.Transport, for the same reason - see its csproj note.

    Encryption at rest uses System.Security.Cryptography (AES-GCM, PBKDF2) from the framework
    rather than a key-derivation package, so the credential store has no dependency that could
    introduce a plaintext path we did not write.
  -->

</Project>
